  "kind": "ode_solution",
  "title": "Integrating factor",
  "prompt": "Solve $x\\,y' + 2y = 4x^2$ for $x > 0$, with $y(1) = 2$.",
  "answer_format": "y = ... in terms of x.",
  "difficulty": 2,
  "tags": [
    "first-order linear",
    "integrating factor",
    "IVP"
  ],
  "data": {
    "ode": "x y' + 2y = 4x^2",
    "ics": {
      "y(1)": "2"
    },
    "variables": {
      "x": "positive"
    }
  },
  "answer": "y = x^2 + 1/x^2",
  "mistakes": [],
  "hints": [
    "Divide by x to get the standard form y′ + P(x) y = Q(x).",
    "The integrating factor is μ = e^(∫P dx) = x²."
  ],
  "solution": [
    "Standard form: $y' + \\frac{2}{x}y = 4x$; $\\mu = e^{\\int 2/x\\,dx} = x^2$.",
    "$(x^2 y)' = 4x^3 \\Rightarrow x^2 y = x^4 + C \\Rightarrow y = x^2 + \\frac{C}{x^2}$.",
    "$y(1) = 1 + C = 2$ gives $C = 1$: $y = x^2 + \\frac{1}{x^2}$."
  ],
  "reference": {
    "book": "openstax-calc2",
    "section": "4.5 First-order Linear Equations",
    "note": "Original problem."
  },
  "review": {
    "status": "draft"
  },
